/-!
# Rockafellar, Section 5: Functional operations

This module formalizes Section 5 of [rockafellar1970convex]: the operations that construct new
convex functions from given ones. They are the composition $\varphi \circ f$ with a non-decreasing
convex function $\varphi$, addition, the lower boundary $f(x) = \inf \{\mu \mid (x, \mu) \in F\}$ of
a convex set $F \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{n+1}$, infimal convolution
$$(f_1 \mathbin{\square} \dots \mathbin{\square} f_m)(x) = \inf \{f_1(x_1) + \dots + f_m(x_m) \mid
  x_1 + \dots + x_m = x\},$$
left and right scalar multiplication, the positively homogeneous convex function generated by a
function, pointwise suprema, the convex hull of a function and of a collection of functions, and the
image $Ah$ and the inverse image $gA$ under a linear map $A$. The proofs specialize the backbone
modules `Numlib.Analysis.Convex.Operations.InfConv`, `Numlib.Analysis.Convex.Operations.Image`,
`Numlib.Analysis.Convex.Homogenize` and `Numlib.Analysis.Convex.Lattice`.

All 8 numbered results of Section 5 are formalized.

## Main results

* Theorem 5.1, `Rockafellar.theorem_5_1`: $\varphi \circ f$ is convex for a convex $f$ and a
  non-decreasing convex $\varphi$.
* Theorem 5.2, `Rockafellar.theorem_5_2`: the sum of two proper convex functions is convex.
* Theorem 5.3, `Rockafellar.theorem_5_3`: the lower boundary
  $f(x) = \inf \{\mu \mid (x, \mu) \in F\}$ of a convex set $F$ in $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ is a convex
  function.
* Theorem 5.4, `Rockafellar.theorem_5_4`: the infimal convolute $f_1 \mathbin{\square} \dots
  \mathbin{\square} f_m$ of proper convex functions is convex.
* Theorem 5.5, `Rockafellar.theorem_5_5`: the pointwise supremum of an arbitrary collection of
  convex functions is convex.
* Theorem 5.6, `Rockafellar.theorem_5_6`: the convex hull of a collection of proper convex functions
  $f_i$ is $f(x) = \inf \{\sum_i \lambda_i f_i(x_i) \mid \sum_i \lambda_i x_i = x\}$ over convex
  combinations.
* Theorem 5.7, `Rockafellar.theorem_5_7_gA`, `Rockafellar.theorem_5_7_Ah`: the inverse image
  $(gA)(x) = g(Ax)$ and the image $(Ah)(y) = \inf \{h(x) \mid Ax = y\}$ of a convex function under a
  linear map are convex.
* Theorem 5.8, `Rockafellar.theorem_5_8_f`, `Rockafellar.theorem_5_8_g`,
  `Rockafellar.theorem_5_8_h`, `Rockafellar.theorem_5_8_k`: four functions constructed from convex
  functions $f_1, \dots, f_m$ by infima over decompositions of $x$ and of the scalar multipliers are
  convex.

## Notation

* `f □ g`: the infimal convolution `ConvexAnalysis.infConv f g`, a scoped notation in the namespace
  `Rockafellar`.

## Conventions

* Theorems 5.7 and 5.8 have no part labels in the book, so the declaration names use the symbols of
  the book: `gA` and `Ah` for Theorem 5.7, and the four function names `f`, `g`, `h`, `k` of Theorem
  5.8.
* The function $f0$ is defined by cases, as in the book: $f0 = \delta(\cdot \mid 0)$ if
  $f \not\equiv +\infty$ (`Rockafellar.rightSMul_zero`), and $f0 = f$ if $f \equiv +\infty$
  (`Rockafellar.rightSMul_zero_of_top`). The backbone result `ConvexAnalysis.hom_apply_smul` shows
  that this case distinction agrees with the convention $0 \cdot \infty = 0$ of Section 4.

## Implementation notes

* The space $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ is the product `Rn n × ℝ`. This is the meaning of "a convex set $F$
  in $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$" in Theorem 5.3, which writes the points of $F$ as $(x, \mu)$.
* Infimal convolution does not preserve properness. For $v \neq 0$, the functions
  $f(x) = \langle v, x \rangle$ and $g(x) = -\langle v, x \rangle$ are proper and convex, and
  $f \mathbin{\square} g \equiv -\infty$; see
  `Rockafellar.exists_not_properConvex_infimalConvolution`. No declaration here concludes that an
  infimal convolute is proper.
* For the same reason the backbone defines $f \mathbin{\square} g$ by addition of epigraphs, not by
  the infimum formula, which would contain $\infty - \infty$ when a function takes the value
  $-\infty$. In this form, $f \mathbin{\square} g$ is convex for all convex $f$ and $g$
  (`Rockafellar.convexFn_infimalConvolution`), and `Rockafellar.infimalConvolution_apply` gives the
  infimum formula for proper $f$ and $g$.
* The linear maps `Rockafellar.sumLin`, `Rockafellar.homSumLin`, `Rockafellar.coordHomLin`,
  `Rockafellar.sumPairLin` and `Rockafellar.coordPairLin` express the operations of Theorems 5.4 and
  5.8 as images and inverse images in the sense of Theorem 5.7. `Rockafellar.coordFunctional` is the
  coordinate functional $x \mapsto \xi_j$ used in the examples after Theorem 5.5.

## Deviations from the source

* **Theorem 5.1** (generalization). The book takes $\varphi$ with values in $(-\infty, +\infty]$.
  The statement allows $\varphi$ to take the value $-\infty$. See `Rockafellar.theorem_5_1`.
* **Section 5, infimal convolution** (generalization). With the epigraph definition, the domain
  sum and translation formulas also hold for improper and nonconvex functions. See
  `Rockafellar.convexDom_infimalConvolution` and
  `Rockafellar.infimalConvolution_indicatorFn_singleton`.
* **Section 5, right scalar multiplication** (generalization). The lower boundary of the scaled
  epigraph is convex for every real scale and the formulas at nonnegative scales hold for
  arbitrary functions. See `Rockafellar.convexFn_rightSMul`, `Rockafellar.rightSMul_apply_pos` and
  `Rockafellar.rightSMul_zero`.
* **Section 5, positively homogeneous convex generator** (generalization). The greatest-function
  property holds for arbitrary $h$, and the formula away from $0$ includes $h \equiv +\infty$.
  See `Rockafellar.isGreatest_posHomGen` and `Rockafellar.posHomGen_apply`.
* **Section 5, maximum coordinate and Tchebycheff norm** (generalization). The extended-real
  supremum equals $-\infty$ when there are no coordinates. See `Rockafellar.convexFn_maxCoord`
  and `Rockafellar.convexFn_tchebycheffNorm`.
* **Theorem 5.8**, function $f$ (generalization). The book assumes that $f_1, \dots, f_m$ are
  proper. The statement does not. See `Rockafellar.theorem_5_8_f`.
* **Theorem 5.8**, function $h$ (generalization). The book assumes that $f_1, \dots, f_m$ are
  proper. The statement does not. See `Rockafellar.theorem_5_8_h`.
-/

open Set Pointwise

namespace Rockafellar

open ConvexAnalysis

/-!
### Linear functionals on $\mathbb{R}^n$

The examples of this section need the coordinate functionals of $\mathbb{R}^n$. The backbone, which
is written for a general topological vector space, does not provide them.
-/

/-- The $j$-th coordinate functional of $\mathbb{R}^n$: the linear map $x \mapsto \xi_j$, where
$x = (\xi_1, \dots, \xi_n)$. -/
noncomputable def coordFunctional (n : ℕ) (j : Fin n) : Rn n →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ where
  toFun x := x j
  map_add' _ _ := rfl
  map_smul' _ _ := rfl

/-- The $j$-th coordinate functional maps $x$ to its $j$-th coordinate. -/
@[simp] theorem coordFunctional_apply {n : ℕ} (j : Fin n) (x : Rn n) :
    coordFunctional n j x = x j := rfl

/-! ### Theorem 5.1 -/

/-- **Theorem 5.1** of [rockafellar1970convex]. Let $f$ be a convex function from $\mathbb{R}^n$ to
$(-\infty, +\infty]$, and let $\varphi$ be a convex function from $\mathbb{R}$ to
$[-\infty, +\infty]$ which is non-decreasing. Then $h(x) = \varphi(f(x))$ is convex on
$\mathbb{R}^n$, where one sets $\varphi(+\infty) = +\infty$.

**Generalization.** The book takes $\varphi$ with values in $(-\infty, +\infty]$. The statement
has no hypothesis on the values of $\varphi$.

The extension of $\varphi$ to $[-\infty, +\infty]$ is `ConvexAnalysis.extendTop φ`, with
$\varphi(+\infty) = +\infty$ and $\varphi(-\infty) = -\infty$. The value at $-\infty$ is never
used, because $f$ does not take the value $-\infty$. -/
theorem theorem_5_1 {n : ℕ} {f : Rn n → EReal} {φ : ℝ → EReal} (hf : ConvexFn f)
    (hf' : ∀ x, f x ≠ ⊥) (hφ : ConvexFn φ) (hmono : Monotone φ) :
    ConvexFn fun x => extendTop φ (f x) :=
  hf.comp_extendTop hf' hφ hmono

/-! ### Theorem 5.2 -/

/-- **Theorem 5.2** of [rockafellar1970convex]. If $f_1$ and $f_2$ are proper convex functions on
$\mathbb{R}^n$, then $f_1 + f_2$ is convex.

Properness excludes the undefined sum $\infty - \infty$. The proof uses only the part of
properness that says $f_i$ never takes the value $-\infty$. -/
theorem theorem_5_2 {n : ℕ} {f₁ f₂ : Rn n → EReal} (h₁ : ConvexFn f₁) (hp₁ : ProperConvex f₁)
    (h₂ : ConvexFn f₂) (hp₂ : ProperConvex f₂) : ConvexFn (f₁ + f₂) :=
  h₁.add h₂ hp₁.ne_bot hp₂.ne_bot

/-! ### Theorem 5.3 -/

/-- **Theorem 5.3** of [rockafellar1970convex]. Let $F$ be any convex set in $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$, and
let
$$f(x) = \inf \{\mu \mid (x, \mu) \in F\}.$$
Then $f$ is a convex function on $\mathbb{R}^n$.

The space $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ is `Rn n × ℝ`. The infimum is taken in `EReal`, so $f(x) = +\infty$
when no $(x, \mu)$ lies in $F$. -/
theorem theorem_5_3 {n : ℕ} {F : Set (Rn n × ℝ)} (hF : Convex ℝ F) {f : Rn n → EReal}
    (hf : ∀ x, f x = ⨅ μ ∈ {μ : ℝ | (x, μ) ∈ F}, (μ : EReal)) : ConvexFn f := by
  have hfF : f = ofEpi F := funext hf
  rw [hfF]
  exact convexFn_ofEpi hF

/-! ### Theorem 5.4 -/

/-- Let $g$ be a convex function on $\mathbb{R}^n$ and $1 \le i \le m$. Then
$(x_1, \dots, x_m) \mapsto g(x_i)$ is a convex function on $(\mathbb{R}^n)^m$. This is the
inverse image of $g$ under the $i$-th projection, as in Theorem 5.7. -/
theorem convexFn_coord {n m : ℕ} {g : Rn n → EReal} (hg : ConvexFn g) (i : Fin m) :
    ConvexFn fun p : Fin m → Rn n => g (p i) := by
  have h := convexFn_compLin (E := Fin m → Rn n) (G := Rn n)
    (LinearMap.proj (R := ℝ) (φ := fun _ : Fin m => Rn n) i) hg
  exact h

/-- The linear map $(x_1, \dots, x_m) \mapsto x_1 + \dots + x_m$ from $(\mathbb{R}^n)^m$ to
$\mathbb{R}^n$. Every operation of this section that adds in $x$ is an image under this map, in
the sense of Theorem 5.7. -/
noncomputable def sumLin (n m : ℕ) : (Fin m → Rn n) →ₗ[ℝ] Rn n :=
  ∑ i, LinearMap.proj i

/-- `Rockafellar.sumLin` maps $(x_1, \dots, x_m)$ to $x_1 + \dots + x_m$. -/
@[simp] theorem sumLin_apply {n m : ℕ} (p : Fin m → Rn n) : sumLin n m p = ∑ i, p i := by
  simp [sumLin, LinearMap.sum_apply]

/-- **Theorem 5.4** of [rockafellar1970convex]. Let $f_1, \dots, f_m$ be proper convex functions on
$\mathbb{R}^n$, and let
$$f(x) = \inf \{f_1(x_1) + \dots + f_m(x_m) \mid x_i \in \mathbb{R}^n,\ x_1 + \dots + x_m =
  x\}.$$
Then $f$ is a convex function on $\mathbb{R}^n$.

The proof uses only the part of properness that says $f_i$ never takes the value $-\infty$,
which makes the sum well defined. -/
theorem theorem_5_4 {n m : ℕ} {f : Fin m → Rn n → EReal} (hf : ∀ i, ConvexFn (f i))
    (hp : ∀ i, ProperConvex (f i)) :
    ConvexFn fun x => ⨅ p ∈ {p : Fin m → Rn n | ∑ i, p i = x}, ∑ i, f i (p i) := by
  have hsep : ConvexFn fun p : Fin m → Rn n => ∑ i, f i (p i) :=
    ConvexFn.sum (s := (Finset.univ : Finset (Fin m)))
      (fun i _ => convexFn_coord (hf i) i) (fun i _ p => (hp i).ne_bot (p i))
  have hmap := convexFn_mapLin (sumLin n m) hsep
  have hkey : mapLin (sumLin n m) (fun p : Fin m → Rn n => ∑ i, f i (p i))
      = fun x => ⨅ p ∈ {p : Fin m → Rn n | ∑ i, p i = x}, ∑ i, f i (p i) := by
    funext x
    simp only [mapLin, sumLin_apply]
  rwa [hkey] at hmap

/-!
### Infimal convolution

After Theorem 5.4, [rockafellar1970convex] writes: "The function $f$ in Theorem 5.4 will be denoted
by $f_1 \mathbin{\square} f_2 \mathbin{\square} \cdots \mathbin{\square} f_m$. The operation
$\square$ is called infimal convolution."
-/

@[inherit_doc ConvexAnalysis.infConv]
scoped infixl:65 " □ " => ConvexAnalysis.infConv

/-- **Theorem 5.4** of [rockafellar1970convex], the case of two functions. If $f$ and $g$ are convex
functions on $\mathbb{R}^n$, then $f \mathbin{\square} g$ is convex.

No properness is needed, because $f \mathbin{\square} g$ is defined as the function with lower
boundary $\operatorname{epi} f + \operatorname{epi} g$, and this is Theorem 5.3 applied to a sum
of convex sets. Properness in the book makes the infimum formula meaningful. -/
theorem convexFn_infimalConvolution {n : ℕ} {f g : Rn n → EReal} (hf : ConvexFn f)
    (hg : ConvexFn g) : ConvexFn (f □ g) :=
  convexFn_infConv hf hg

/-- Let $f$ and $g$ be proper functions on $\mathbb{R}^n$. Then for every $x$,
$$(f \mathbin{\square} g)(x) = \inf_y \{f(x - y) + g(y)\}.$$

This is the analogue of the classical formula for integral convolution. Properness makes the
right-hand side well defined. The backbone defines $\square$ through addition of epigraphs, as
the book does, because this infimum contains the undefined sum $\infty - \infty$ when a function
takes the value $-\infty$. -/
theorem infimalConvolution_apply {n : ℕ} {f g : Rn n → EReal} (hf : ProperConvex f)
    (hg : ProperConvex g)
    (x : Rn n) : (f □ g) x = ⨅ y, f (x - y) + g y :=
  infConv_apply hf.ne_bot hg.ne_bot x

/-- **Section 5** of [rockafellar1970convex]. "The effective domain of $f \mathbin{\square} g$ is
the sum of $\operatorname{dom} f$ and $\operatorname{dom} g$": for all functions $f, g$ on
$\mathbb{R}^n$,
$$\operatorname{dom}(f \mathbin{\square} g) = \operatorname{dom} f + \operatorname{dom} g.$$

**Generalization.** The book states this identity in its discussion of infimal convolution of
proper convex functions. The effective domain is the projection of the epigraph, so addition of
epigraphs gives the domain formula for arbitrary functions. -/
theorem convexDom_infimalConvolution {n : ℕ} (f g : Rn n → EReal) :
    convexDom (f □ g) = convexDom f + convexDom g :=
  convexDom_infConv f g

/-- **Section 5** of [rockafellar1970convex]. For a function $f$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$ and
$a \in \mathbb{R}^n$, $f \mathbin{\square} \delta(\cdot \mid a)$ is the function whose graph is
obtained by translating the graph of $f$ horizontally by $a$:
$$(f \mathbin{\square} \delta(\cdot \mid a))(x) = f(x - a).$$

**Generalization.** The book gives the formula in its discussion of proper convex functions.
Adding the epigraph of a singleton indicator translates the lower boundary of any epigraph. -/
theorem infimalConvolution_indicatorFn_singleton {n : ℕ} (f : Rn n → EReal) (a : Rn n) :
    (f □ indicatorFn ({a} : Set (Rn n))) = fun x => f (x - a) :=
  infConv_indicatorFn_singleton f a

/-- Infimal convolution of functions $\mathbb{R}^n \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ is commutative and
associative, and $\delta(\cdot \mid 0)$ is its identity:
$$f \mathbin{\square} g = g \mathbin{\square} f, \qquad (f \mathbin{\square} g)
  \mathbin{\square} h = f \mathbin{\square} (g \mathbin{\square} h), \qquad f \mathbin{\square}
  \delta(\cdot \mid 0) = f.$$

The backbone puts this commutative monoid structure on the type synonym
`ConvexAnalysis.InfConvFn (Rn n)`. -/
theorem infimalConvolution_isCommMonoid {n : ℕ} :
    (∀ f g : Rn n → EReal, (f □ g) = g □ f) ∧
      (∀ f g h : Rn n → EReal, ((f □ g) □ h) = f □ (g □ h)) ∧
      (∀ f : Rn n → EReal, (f □ indicatorFn ({0} : Set (Rn n))) = f) :=
  ⟨infConv_comm, infConv_assoc, infConv_indicatorFn_zero⟩

/-- Infimal convolution does not preserve properness. Let $v \in \mathbb{R}^n$ with $v \neq 0$. Then
there are proper convex functions $f$ and $g$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$ such that
$f \mathbin{\square} g$ is not proper.

The witnesses are $f(x) = \langle v, x \rangle$ and $g(x) = -\langle v, x \rangle$, which are
finite and convex, while $(f \mathbin{\square} g)(x) = -\infty$ for every $x$. This is why no
result of Section 5 concludes that an infimal convolute is proper. -/
theorem exists_not_properConvex_infimalConvolution {n : ℕ} {v : Rn n} (hv : v ≠ 0) :
    ∃ f g : Rn n → EReal,
        ConvexFn f ∧ ProperConvex f ∧ ConvexFn g ∧ ProperConvex g ∧ ¬ ProperConvex (f □ g) := by
  have hsurj : Function.Surjective (pairing n v) := by
    refine LinearMap.surjective_of_ne_zero ?_
    intro hzero
    refine hv ((inner_self_eq_zero (𝕜 := ℝ)).1 ?_)
    have hvv := congrArg (fun t : Rn n →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ => t v) hzero
    simpa using hvv
  have hbot : infConv (fun x : Rn n => ((pairing n v x : ℝ) : EReal))
      (fun x : Rn n => ((-(pairing n v x) : ℝ) : EReal)) 0 = ⊥ := by
    rw [infConv_apply (fun x => by simp) (fun x => by simp) 0]
    refine le_bot_iff.1 (EReal.le_of_forall_lt_iff_le.1 fun r _ => ?_)
    obtain ⟨y, hy⟩ := hsurj (-r / 2)
    refine le_trans (iInf_le _ y) (le_of_eq ?_)
    rw [← EReal.coe_add, EReal.coe_eq_coe_iff, map_sub, map_zero, hy]
    ring
  exact ⟨_, _, convexFn_coe_linearMap (pairing n v), ⟨⟨0, by simp⟩, fun x => by simp⟩,
    convexFn_coe_linearMap (-(pairing n v)), ⟨⟨0, by simp⟩, fun x => by simp⟩,
    fun hproper => hproper.ne_bot 0 hbot⟩

/-!
### Left and right scalar multiplication

In [rockafellar1970convex], Section 5, $(\lambda f)(x) = \lambda f(x)$ for $\lambda \ge 0$, and
$f\lambda$ is the function obtained from Theorem 5.3 with $F = \lambda (\operatorname{epi} f)$.
-/

/-- Let $f$ be a convex function on $\mathbb{R}^n$ and $\lambda \ge 0$. Then the left scalar
multiple $(\lambda f)(x) = \lambda f(x)$ is convex.

The type `EReal` satisfies the convention $0 \cdot \infty = 0$ of the book, so $\lambda = 0$ is
not a special case. -/
theorem convexFn_leftSMul {n : ℕ} {f : Rn n → EReal} (l : ℝ) (hl : 0 ≤ l) (hf : ConvexFn f) :
    ConvexFn fun x => (l : EReal) * f x :=
  convexFn_coe_mul hl hf

/-- **Section 5** of [rockafellar1970convex]. Right scalar multiplication preserves convexity: if
$f$ is a convex function on $\mathbb{R}^n$, then $f\lambda$ is convex for every real $\lambda$.

**Generalization.** The book defines right scalar multiplication for $\lambda \ge 0$. The scaled
epigraph is convex for every real $\lambda$, so its lower boundary is convex by Theorem 5.3.

The function $f\lambda$ is `ConvexAnalysis.smulRight f λ`. -/
theorem convexFn_rightSMul {n : ℕ} {f : Rn n → EReal} (l : ℝ) (hf : ConvexFn f) :
    ConvexFn (smulRight f l) :=
  convexFn_smulRight l hf

/-- **Section 5** of [rockafellar1970convex], definition of $f\lambda$ for $\lambda > 0$. For every
function $f$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$ and $\lambda > 0$,
$$(f\lambda)(x) = \lambda f(\lambda^{-1} x).$$

**Generalization.** The book defines the operation for convex $f$. For every function, positive
scaling sends the epigraph to the epigraph of the stated function. -/
theorem rightSMul_apply_pos {n : ℕ} {l : ℝ} (hl : 0 < l) (f : Rn n → EReal) (x : Rn n) :
    smulRight f l x = (l : EReal) * f (l⁻¹ • x) :=
  smulRight_apply_pos hl f x

/-- **Section 5** of [rockafellar1970convex], definition of $f0$. If $f \not\equiv +\infty$, then
$f0 = \delta(\cdot \mid 0)$.

**Generalization.** The book assumes convexity. Every function with a nonempty effective domain
has a nonempty epigraph, so scaling it by zero reduces it to the origin. -/
theorem rightSMul_zero {n : ℕ} {f : Rn n → EReal} (hf : (convexDom f).Nonempty) :
    smulRight f 0 = indicatorFn ({0} : Set (Rn n)) :=
  smulRight_zero hf

/-- **Section 5** of [rockafellar1970convex], definition of $f0$ when $f \equiv +\infty$. Then
$f0 = f$. With `Rockafellar.rightSMul_zero`, this gives the whole definition by cases of the book
at $\lambda = 0$. -/
theorem rightSMul_zero_of_top {n : ℕ} {f : Rn n → EReal} (hf : f = ⊤) :
    smulRight f 0 = f := by
  rw [smulRight_of_epi_eq_empty (by rw [hf]; exact epi_top) 0, hf]

/-- **Section 5** of [rockafellar1970convex]. "A function $f$ is positively homogeneous if and only
if $f\lambda = f$ for every $\lambda > 0$." -/
theorem posHomogeneous_iff_rightSMul_eq {n : ℕ} {f : Rn n → EReal} :
    PosHomogeneous f ↔ ∀ l > (0 : ℝ), smulRight f l = f :=
  posHomogeneous_iff_smulRight_eq

/-! ### The positively homogeneous convex function generated by a function -/

/-- **Section 5** of [rockafellar1970convex], the positively homogeneous convex function generated
by $h$. For every function $h$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$, the function `ConvexAnalysis.posHomGen h` is
the greatest function $f$ such that $f$ is positively homogeneous and convex, $f(0) \le 0$ and
$f \le h$.

**Generalization.** The book assumes that $h$ is convex. The greatest-function property holds
for every $h$ since the construction takes the convex cone generated by its epigraph.

The function is obtained by Theorem 5.3 from the convex cone in $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ generated by
$\operatorname{epi} h$. It is not the backbone function `ConvexAnalysis.hom`, which is the same
construction applied to the lift of $h$ at level one, and is a function on
$\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}^n$. -/
theorem isGreatest_posHomGen {n : ℕ} (h : Rn n → EReal) :
    IsGreatest {f : Rn n → EReal | PosHomogeneous f ∧ ConvexFn f ∧ f 0 ≤ 0 ∧ f ≤ h}
      (posHomGen h) :=
  posHomGen_isGreatest h

/-- **Section 5** of [rockafellar1970convex], formula for the positively homogeneous convex function
generated by $h$. Let $h$ be a convex function on $\mathbb{R}^n$, let $f$ be the positively
homogeneous convex function generated by $h$, and let $x \neq 0$. Then
$$f(x) = \inf \{(h\lambda)(x) \mid \lambda > 0\}.$$

**Generalization.** The book gives the infimum formula when $h \not\equiv +\infty$. At $x \neq 0$
the formula over $\lambda > 0$ also holds for $h \equiv +\infty$, since every term and the
generated function at $x$ are then $+\infty$. -/
theorem posHomGen_apply {n : ℕ} {h : Rn n → EReal} (hh : ConvexFn h) {x : Rn n} (hx : x ≠ 0) :
    posHomGen h x = ⨅ l > (0 : ℝ), smulRight h l x := by
  rw [posHomGen_apply_of_ne_zero hh hx]
  exact iInf_congr fun l => iInf_congr fun hl => (smulRight_apply_pos hl h x).symm

/-! ### Theorem 5.5 -/

/-- **Theorem 5.5** of [rockafellar1970convex]. The pointwise supremum of an arbitrary collection
$(f_i)_{i \in I}$ of convex functions on $\mathbb{R}^n$ is convex: $x \mapsto \sup_i f_i(x)$ is
a convex function. -/
theorem theorem_5_5 {n : ℕ} {ι : Sort*} {f : ι → Rn n → EReal} (hf : ∀ i, ConvexFn (f i)) :
    ConvexFn fun x => ⨆ i, f i x :=
  convexFn_iSup hf

/-- **Section 5** of [rockafellar1970convex], the example after Theorem 5.5. The function that
assigns to $x = (\xi_1, \dots, \xi_n)$ the greatest of its components $\xi_j$ is convex,
"because it is the pointwise supremum of the linear functions $\langle x, e_j \rangle$".

**Generalization.** The book takes the maximum of a nonempty coordinate family. The supremum in
`EReal` also gives a convex function when $n = 0$, where its value is $-\infty$.

The function is also the support function of the unit simplex. -/
theorem convexFn_maxCoord {n : ℕ} :
    ConvexFn fun x : Rn n => ⨆ j : Fin n, ((x j : ℝ) : EReal) :=
  convexFn_iSup fun j => convexFn_coe_linearMap (coordFunctional n j)

/-- **Section 5** of [rockafellar1970convex]. The function
$k(x) = \max \{|\xi_j| \mid j = 1, \dots, n\}$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$, "which is called the
**Tchebycheff norm** on $\mathbb{R}^n$", is convex, as "can be seen similarly from Theorem 5.5":
it is the pointwise supremum of the $2n$ linear functions $\pm \xi_j$.

**Generalization.** The book uses the maximum over a nonempty coordinate family. For $n = 0$ the
extended-real supremum gives the constant $-\infty$, which is convex. -/
theorem convexFn_tchebycheffNorm {n : ℕ} :
    ConvexFn fun x : Rn n => ⨆ j : Fin n, ((|x j| : ℝ) : EReal) := by
  have hrw : (fun x : Rn n => ⨆ j : Fin n, ((|x j| : ℝ) : EReal))
      = fun x : Rn n => ⨆ p : Fin n × Bool, ((cond p.2 (x p.1) (-(x p.1)) : ℝ) : EReal) := by
    funext x
    rw [iSup_prod]
    refine iSup_congr fun j => ?_
    simp only [abs_eq_max_neg, iSup_bool_eq, Bool.cond_true, Bool.cond_false]
    exact_mod_cast rfl
  rw [hrw]
  refine convexFn_iSup fun p => ?_
  obtain ⟨j, b⟩ := p
  cases b
  · exact convexFn_coe_linearMap (-coordFunctional n j)
  · exact convexFn_coe_linearMap (coordFunctional n j)

/-!
### The convex hull of a function, and of a collection

The convex hull $\operatorname{conv} g$ is the function obtained from Theorem 5.3 with
$F = \operatorname{conv}(\operatorname{epi} g)$. The convex hull $\operatorname{conv} \{f_i\}$ of a
collection is obtained in the same way from the convex hull of the union of the epigraphs.
-/

/-- **Section 5** of [rockafellar1970convex]. For every function $g$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$,
$\operatorname{conv} g$ "is the greatest convex function majorized by $g$".

This restates the backbone result `ConvexAnalysis.isGreatest_convHullFn`. -/
theorem isGreatest_conv {n : ℕ} (g : Rn n → EReal) :
    IsGreatest {h : Rn n → EReal | ConvexFn h ∧ h ≤ g} (convHullFn g) :=
  isGreatest_convHullFn g

/-- **Section 5** of [rockafellar1970convex]. For every collection $(f_i)_{i \in I}$ of functions on
$\mathbb{R}^n$, $\operatorname{conv} \{f_i \mid i \in I\}$ "is the greatest convex function $f$
(not necessarily proper) on $\mathbb{R}^n$ such that $f(x) \le f_i(x)$ for every
$x \in \mathbb{R}^n$ and every $i \in I$".

This restates the backbone result `ConvexAnalysis.isGreatest_convFn`. -/
theorem isGreatest_convCollection {n : ℕ} {ι : Sort*} (f : ι → Rn n → EReal) :
    IsGreatest {g : Rn n → EReal | ConvexFn g ∧ ∀ i, g ≤ f i} (convFn f) :=
  isGreatest_convFn f

/-! ### Theorem 5.6 -/

/-- **Theorem 5.6** of [rockafellar1970convex]. Let $(f_i)_{i \in I}$ be any collection of proper
convex functions on $\mathbb{R}^n$, and let $f$ be the convex hull of the collection. Then
$$f(x) = \inf \Bigl\{\sum_{i \in I} \lambda_i f_i(x_i) \Bigm| \sum_{i \in I} \lambda_i x_i =
  x\Bigr\},$$
where the infimum is taken over all representations of $x$ as a convex combination of elements
$x_i$, such that only finitely many coefficients $\lambda_i$ are non-zero.

A representation is a finite set $t \subseteq I$, coefficients $\lambda_i \ge 0$ for $i \in t$
with $\sum_{i \in t} \lambda_i = 1$, and points $x_i$ with $\sum_{i \in t} \lambda_i x_i = x$.
This is the analogue for functions of Theorem 2.3. -/
theorem theorem_5_6 {n : ℕ} {ι : Type*} {f : ι → Rn n → EReal} (hf : ∀ i, ConvexFn (f i))
    (hp : ∀ i, ProperConvex (f i)) (x : Rn n) :
    convFn f x = sInf {z : EReal | ∃ (t : Finset ι) (w : ι → ℝ) (p : ι → Rn n),
      (∀ i ∈ t, 0 ≤ w i) ∧ ∑ i ∈ t, w i = 1 ∧ ∑ i ∈ t, w i • p i = x ∧
        z = ∑ i ∈ t, (w i : EReal) * f i (p i)} :=
  convFn_apply hf (fun i y => (hp i).ne_bot y) x

/-- The convex functions on $\mathbb{R}^n$, ordered pointwise, form a complete lattice in which the
greatest lower bound of a collection $\{f_i\}$ is $\operatorname{conv} \{f_i\}$.

The lattice is `ConvexAnalysis.ConvexFns (Rn n)`. The backbone result
`ConvexAnalysis.ConvexFns.not_coe_inf_eq_inf` shows that this greatest lower bound is not the
pointwise infimum, which is the warning "(relative to this particular partially ordered set!)"
of the book. -/
theorem coe_iInf_convexFns {n : ℕ} {ι : Sort*} (f : ι → ConvexFns (Rn n)) :
    ((⨅ i, f i : ConvexFns (Rn n)) : Rn n → EReal) = convFn fun i => (f i : Rn n → EReal) :=
  ConvexFns.coe_iInf f

/-- In the complete lattice of convex functions on $\mathbb{R}^n$, the least upper bound of a
collection $\{f_i\}$ is the pointwise supremum $x \mapsto \sup_i f_i(x)$, which is convex by
Theorem 5.5. -/
theorem coe_iSup_convexFns {n : ℕ} {ι : Sort*} (f : ι → ConvexFns (Rn n)) (x : Rn n) :
    ((⨆ i, f i : ConvexFns (Rn n)) : Rn n → EReal) x = ⨆ i, (f i : Rn n → EReal) x :=
  ConvexFns.coe_iSup_apply f x

/-! ### Theorem 5.7 -/

/-- **Theorem 5.7** of [rockafellar1970convex], the inverse image. Let $A$ be a linear
transformation from $\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\mathbb{R}^m$. Then, for each convex function $g$ on
$\mathbb{R}^m$, the function $gA$ defined by
$$(gA)(x) = g(Ax)$$
is convex on $\mathbb{R}^n$. -/
theorem theorem_5_7_gA {n m : ℕ} (A : Rn n →ₗ[ℝ] Rn m) {g : Rn m → EReal} (hg : ConvexFn g) :
    ConvexFn fun x => g (A x) :=
  convexFn_compLin A hg

/-- **Theorem 5.7** of [rockafellar1970convex], the image. Let $A$ be a linear transformation from
$\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\mathbb{R}^m$. Then, for each convex function $h$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$, the
function $Ah$ defined by
$$(Ah)(y) = \inf \{h(x) \mid Ax = y\}$$
is convex on $\mathbb{R}^m$.

The infimum need not be attained, so $Ah$ is not defined from the image of
$\operatorname{epi} h$ as a set. -/
theorem theorem_5_7_Ah {n m : ℕ} (A : Rn n →ₗ[ℝ] Rn m) {h : Rn n → EReal} (hh : ConvexFn h) :
    ConvexFn fun y => ⨅ x ∈ {x | A x = y}, h x :=
  convexFn_mapLin A hh

/-!
### Theorem 5.8

The book derives the four functions of Theorem 5.8 from partial additions of convex cones in
$\mathbb{R}^{n+2}$. Here each of the four formulas is an instance of Theorem 5.7, applied to a
jointly convex function of auxiliary variables, so the statements do not depend on that
construction.
-/

/-- The linear map $(\lambda, x) \mapsto (\lambda_1 + \dots + \lambda_m, x)$ from
$\mathbb{R}^m \times \mathbb{R}^n$ to $\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}^n$. The two operations of
Theorem 5.8 that add in $\lambda$ are images under this map. -/
noncomputable def homSumLin (n m : ℕ) : ((Fin m → ℝ) × Rn n) →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ × Rn n :=
  LinearMap.prod ((∑ i, LinearMap.proj i).comp (LinearMap.fst ℝ (Fin m → ℝ) (Rn n)))
    (LinearMap.snd ℝ (Fin m → ℝ) (Rn n))

/-- `Rockafellar.homSumLin` maps $(\lambda, x)$ to $(\lambda_1 + \dots + \lambda_m, x)$. -/
@[simp] theorem homSumLin_apply {n m : ℕ} (q : (Fin m → ℝ) × Rn n) :
    homSumLin n m q = (∑ i, q.1 i, q.2) := by
  simp [homSumLin]

/-- The linear map $(\lambda, x) \mapsto (\lambda_i, x)$ from $\mathbb{R}^m \times \mathbb{R}^n$ to
$\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}^n$, which selects the $i$-th homogenizing variable. -/
noncomputable def coordHomLin (n m : ℕ) (i : Fin m) : ((Fin m → ℝ) × Rn n) →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ × Rn n :=
  LinearMap.prod ((LinearMap.proj i).comp (LinearMap.fst ℝ (Fin m → ℝ) (Rn n)))
    (LinearMap.snd ℝ (Fin m → ℝ) (Rn n))

/-- `Rockafellar.coordHomLin` maps $(\lambda, x)$ to $(\lambda_i, x)$. -/
@[simp] theorem coordHomLin_apply {n m : ℕ} (i : Fin m) (q : (Fin m → ℝ) × Rn n) :
    coordHomLin n m i q = (q.1 i, q.2) := rfl

/-- Let $f_1, \dots, f_m$ be convex functions on $\mathbb{R}^n$ and $1 \le i \le m$. Then the
function on $\mathbb{R}^m \times \mathbb{R}^n$ that maps $(\lambda, x)$ to $(f_i \lambda_i)(x)$
for $\lambda_i \ge 0$ and to $+\infty$ for $\lambda_i < 0$ is convex.

This function is the inverse image of `ConvexAnalysis.hom` of $f_i$ under
$(\lambda, x) \mapsto (\lambda_i, x)$. It gives the joint convexity used for the functions $g$
and $h$ of Theorem 5.8. -/
theorem convexFn_hom_coord {n m : ℕ} {f : Fin m → Rn n → EReal} (hf : ∀ i, ConvexFn (f i))
    (i : Fin m) : ConvexFn fun q : (Fin m → ℝ) × Rn n => hom (f i) (q.1 i, q.2) := by
  have h := convexFn_compLin (coordHomLin n m i) (convexFn_hom (hf i))
  exact h

/-- **Theorem 5.8** of [rockafellar1970convex], the function $f$. Let $f_1, \dots, f_m$ be convex
functions on $\mathbb{R}^n$. Then
$$f(x) = \inf \{\max \{f_1(x_1), \dots, f_m(x_m)\} \mid x_1 + \dots + x_m = x\}$$
is a convex function on $\mathbb{R}^n$. This is the operation of adding in $x$ alone.

**Generalization.** The book assumes that $f_1, \dots, f_m$ are proper. The proof does not use
it. -/
theorem theorem_5_8_f {n m : ℕ} {f : Fin m → Rn n → EReal} (hf : ∀ i, ConvexFn (f i)) :
    ConvexFn fun x => ⨅ p ∈ {p : Fin m → Rn n | ∑ i, p i = x}, ⨆ i, f i (p i) := by
  have hsup : ConvexFn fun p : Fin m → Rn n => ⨆ i, f i (p i) :=
    convexFn_iSup fun i => convexFn_coord (hf i) i
  have hmap := convexFn_mapLin (sumLin n m) hsup
  have hkey : mapLin (sumLin n m) (fun p : Fin m → Rn n => ⨆ i, f i (p i))
      = fun x => ⨅ p ∈ {p : Fin m → Rn n | ∑ i, p i = x}, ⨆ i, f i (p i) := by
    funext x
    simp only [mapLin, sumLin_apply]
  rwa [hkey] at hmap

/-- **Theorem 5.8** of [rockafellar1970convex], the function $g$. Let $f_1, \dots, f_m$ be proper
convex functions on $\mathbb{R}^n$. Then
$$g(x) = \inf \{(f_1 \lambda_1)(x) + \dots + (f_m \lambda_m)(x) \mid \lambda_i \ge 0,\
  \lambda_1 + \dots + \lambda_m = 1\}$$
is a convex function on $\mathbb{R}^n$. This is the operation of adding in $\lambda$ and $\mu$.

The formula has the pointwise sum of the functions $f_i \lambda_i$. It differs from the formula
for $\operatorname{conv} \{f_1, \dots, f_m\}$ after Theorem 5.6, which has $f_1 \lambda_1
\mathbin{\square} \dots \mathbin{\square} f_m \lambda_m$ in place of the pointwise sum. -/
theorem theorem_5_8_g {n m : ℕ} {f : Fin m → Rn n → EReal} (hf : ∀ i, ConvexFn (f i))
    (hp : ∀ i, ProperConvex (f i)) :
    ConvexFn fun x => ⨅ l ∈ {l : Fin m → ℝ | (∀ i, 0 ≤ l i) ∧ ∑ i, l i = 1},
      ∑ i, smulRight (f i) (l i) x := by
  have hPsi : ConvexFn fun q : (Fin m → ℝ) × Rn n => ∑ i, hom (f i) (q.1 i, q.2) :=
    ConvexFn.sum (s := (Finset.univ : Finset (Fin m)))
      (fun i _ => convexFn_hom_coord hf i) (fun i _ q => hom_ne_bot (hp i).ne_bot (q.1 i, q.2))
  have hslice := (convexFn_mapLin (homSumLin n m) hPsi).slice_left 1
  have hkey : (fun x : Rn n =>
        mapLin (homSumLin n m) (fun q : (Fin m → ℝ) × Rn n => ∑ i, hom (f i) (q.1 i, q.2)) (1, x))
      = fun x => ⨅ l ∈ {l : Fin m → ℝ | (∀ i, 0 ≤ l i) ∧ ∑ i, l i = 1},
        ∑ i, smulRight (f i) (l i) x := by
    funext x
    refine le_antisymm (le_iInf₂ fun l hl => ?_) (le_mapLin fun q hq => ?_)
    · refine (mapLin_le (x := (l, x)) ?_).trans_eq ?_
      · rw [homSumLin_apply, hl.2]
      · exact Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => hom_apply_nonneg (hl.1 i) (f i) x
    · obtain ⟨l, y⟩ := q
      rw [homSumLin_apply, Prod.mk.injEq] at hq
      obtain ⟨hq₁, rfl⟩ := hq
      by_cases hpos : ∀ i, 0 ≤ l i
      · refine le_trans (iInf₂_le l ⟨hpos, hq₁⟩) (le_of_eq ?_)
        exact Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => (hom_apply_nonneg (hpos i) (f i) y).symm
      · obtain ⟨j, hj⟩ := not_forall.1 hpos
        have hbot : ∀ i ∈ (Finset.univ : Finset (Fin m)), hom (f i) (l i, y) ≠ ⊥ :=
          fun i _ => hom_ne_bot (hp i).ne_bot (l i, y)
        have htop : (∑ i, hom (f i) (l i, y)) = ⊤ := by
          by_contra hne
          exact absurd (hom_apply_neg (not_le.1 hj) (f j) y)
            (EReal.forall_ne_top_of_sum_ne_top _ _ hbot hne j (Finset.mem_univ j))
        rw [htop]
        exact le_top
  rwa [hkey] at hslice

/-- **Theorem 5.8** of [rockafellar1970convex], the function $h$. Let $f_1, \dots, f_m$ be convex
functions on $\mathbb{R}^n$. Then
$$h(x) = \inf \{\max \{(f_1 \lambda_1)(x), \dots, (f_m \lambda_m)(x)\} \mid \lambda_i \ge 0,\
  \lambda_1 + \dots + \lambda_m = 1\}$$
is a convex function on $\mathbb{R}^n$. This is the operation of adding in $\lambda$ alone,
which "amounts to inverse addition of epigraphs".

**Generalization.** The book assumes that $f_1, \dots, f_m$ are proper. The proof does not use
it: a maximum, unlike a sum, cannot produce $\infty - \infty$. -/
theorem theorem_5_8_h {n m : ℕ} {f : Fin m → Rn n → EReal} (hf : ∀ i, ConvexFn (f i)) :
    ConvexFn fun x => ⨅ l ∈ {l : Fin m → ℝ | (∀ i, 0 ≤ l i) ∧ ∑ i, l i = 1},
      ⨆ i, smulRight (f i) (l i) x := by
  have hPsi : ConvexFn fun q : (Fin m → ℝ) × Rn n => ⨆ i, hom (f i) (q.1 i, q.2) :=
    convexFn_iSup fun i => convexFn_hom_coord hf i
  have hslice := (convexFn_mapLin (homSumLin n m) hPsi).slice_left 1
  have hkey : (fun x : Rn n =>
        mapLin (homSumLin n m) (fun q : (Fin m → ℝ) × Rn n => ⨆ i, hom (f i) (q.1 i, q.2)) (1, x))
      = fun x => ⨅ l ∈ {l : Fin m → ℝ | (∀ i, 0 ≤ l i) ∧ ∑ i, l i = 1},
        ⨆ i, smulRight (f i) (l i) x := by
    funext x
    refine le_antisymm (le_iInf₂ fun l hl => ?_) (le_mapLin fun q hq => ?_)
    · refine (mapLin_le (x := (l, x)) ?_).trans_eq ?_
      · rw [homSumLin_apply, hl.2]
      · exact iSup_congr fun i => hom_apply_nonneg (hl.1 i) (f i) x
    · obtain ⟨l, y⟩ := q
      rw [homSumLin_apply, Prod.mk.injEq] at hq
      obtain ⟨hq₁, rfl⟩ := hq
      by_cases hpos : ∀ i, 0 ≤ l i
      · refine le_trans (iInf₂_le l ⟨hpos, hq₁⟩) (le_of_eq ?_)
        exact iSup_congr fun i => (hom_apply_nonneg (hpos i) (f i) y).symm
      · obtain ⟨j, hj⟩ := not_forall.1 hpos
        have htop : (⨆ i, hom (f i) (l i, y)) = ⊤ :=
          top_le_iff.1 (le_iSup_of_le j (hom_apply_neg (not_le.1 hj) (f j) y).ge)
        rw [htop]
        exact le_top
  rwa [hkey] at hslice

/-- The linear map $(\lambda, y) \mapsto (\lambda_1 + \dots + \lambda_m, y_1 + \dots + y_m)$ from
$\mathbb{R}^m \times (\mathbb{R}^n)^m$ to $\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}^n$. The last operation
of Theorem 5.8, adding in $\lambda$ and $x$, is an image under this map. -/
noncomputable def sumPairLin (n m : ℕ) : ((Fin m → ℝ) × (Fin m → Rn n)) →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ × Rn n :=
  LinearMap.prod ((∑ i, LinearMap.proj i).comp (LinearMap.fst ℝ (Fin m → ℝ) (Fin m → Rn n)))
    ((∑ i, LinearMap.proj i).comp (LinearMap.snd ℝ (Fin m → ℝ) (Fin m → Rn n)))

/-- `Rockafellar.sumPairLin` maps $(\lambda, y)$ to $(\lambda_1 + \dots + \lambda_m, y_1 + \dots +
y_m)$. -/
@[simp] theorem sumPairLin_apply {n m : ℕ} (q : (Fin m → ℝ) × (Fin m → Rn n)) :
    sumPairLin n m q = (∑ i, q.1 i, ∑ i, q.2 i) := by
  simp [sumPairLin]

/-- The linear map $(\lambda, y) \mapsto (\lambda_i, y_i)$ from
$\mathbb{R}^m \times (\mathbb{R}^n)^m$ to $\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}^n$. -/
noncomputable def coordPairLin (n m : ℕ) (i : Fin m) :
    ((Fin m → ℝ) × (Fin m → Rn n)) →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ × Rn n :=
  LinearMap.prod ((LinearMap.proj i).comp (LinearMap.fst ℝ (Fin m → ℝ) (Fin m → Rn n)))
    ((LinearMap.proj i).comp (LinearMap.snd ℝ (Fin m → ℝ) (Fin m → Rn n)))

/-- `Rockafellar.coordPairLin` maps $(\lambda, y)$ to $(\lambda_i, y_i)$. -/
@[simp] theorem coordPairLin_apply {n m : ℕ} (i : Fin m) (q : (Fin m → ℝ) × (Fin m → Rn n)) :
    coordPairLin n m i q = (q.1 i, q.2 i) := rfl

/-- **Theorem 5.8** of [rockafellar1970convex], the function $k$. Let $f_1, \dots, f_m$ be proper
convex functions on $\mathbb{R}^n$. Then
$$k(x) = \inf \{\max \{\lambda_1 f_1(x_1), \dots, \lambda_m f_m(x_m)\}\},$$
where the infimum is over all expressions of $x$ as a convex combination
$x = \lambda_1 x_1 + \dots + \lambda_m x_m$ with $\lambda_i \ge 0$ and
$\lambda_1 + \dots + \lambda_m = 1$, is a convex function on $\mathbb{R}^n$. This is the
operation of adding in $\lambda$ and $x$. -/
theorem theorem_5_8_k {n m : ℕ} {f : Fin m → Rn n → EReal} (hf : ∀ i, ConvexFn (f i))
    (hp : ∀ i, ProperConvex (f i)) :
    ConvexFn fun x => ⨅ lp ∈ {lp : (Fin m → ℝ) × (Fin m → Rn n) |
        (∀ i, 0 ≤ lp.1 i) ∧ ∑ i, lp.1 i = 1 ∧ ∑ i, lp.1 i • lp.2 i = x},
      ⨆ i, (lp.1 i : EReal) * f i (lp.2 i) := by
  have hPsi : ConvexFn fun q : (Fin m → ℝ) × (Fin m → Rn n) => ⨆ i, hom (f i) (q.1 i, q.2 i) :=
    convexFn_iSup fun i => by
      have h := convexFn_compLin (coordPairLin n m i) (convexFn_hom (hf i))
      exact h
  have hslice := (convexFn_mapLin (sumPairLin n m) hPsi).slice_left 1
  have hkey : (fun x : Rn n => mapLin (sumPairLin n m)
        (fun q : (Fin m → ℝ) × (Fin m → Rn n) => ⨆ i, hom (f i) (q.1 i, q.2 i)) (1, x))
      = fun x => ⨅ lp ∈ {lp : (Fin m → ℝ) × (Fin m → Rn n) |
          (∀ i, 0 ≤ lp.1 i) ∧ ∑ i, lp.1 i = 1 ∧ ∑ i, lp.1 i • lp.2 i = x},
        ⨆ i, (lp.1 i : EReal) * f i (lp.2 i) := by
    funext x
    refine le_antisymm (le_iInf₂ fun lp hlp => ?_) (le_mapLin fun q hq => ?_)
    · refine (mapLin_le (x := (lp.1, fun i => lp.1 i • lp.2 i)) ?_).trans_eq ?_
      · rw [sumPairLin_apply, hlp.2.1, hlp.2.2]
      · exact iSup_congr fun i => hom_apply_smul (hp i).convexDom_nonempty (hlp.1 i) (lp.2 i)
    · obtain ⟨l, y⟩ := q
      rw [sumPairLin_apply, Prod.mk.injEq] at hq
      obtain ⟨hq₁, hq₂⟩ := hq
      by_cases hpos : ∀ i, 0 ≤ l i
      · by_cases hzero : ∀ i, l i = 0 → y i = 0
        · have hsmul : ∀ i, l i • (l i)⁻¹ • y i = y i := by
            intro i
            rcases eq_or_lt_of_le (hpos i) with h | h
            · rw [← h, zero_smul, hzero i h.symm]
            · rw [smul_smul, mul_inv_cancel₀ h.ne', one_smul]
          refine le_trans (iInf₂_le (l, fun i => (l i)⁻¹ • y i) ⟨hpos, hq₁, ?_⟩) (le_of_eq ?_)
          · refine iSup_congr fun i => ?_
            calc (l i : EReal) * f i ((l i)⁻¹ • y i)
                = hom (f i) (l i, l i • (l i)⁻¹ • y i) :=
                  (hom_apply_smul (hp i).convexDom_nonempty (hpos i) ((l i)⁻¹ • y i)).symm
              _ = hom (f i) (l i, y i) := by rw [hsmul i]
          · change (∑ i, l i • (l i)⁻¹ • y i) = x
            simp_rw [hsmul]
            exact hq₂
        · obtain ⟨j, hj⟩ := not_forall.1 hzero
          have hj0 : l j = 0 := by
            by_contra hc
            exact hj fun h0 => absurd h0 hc
          have hjy : y j ≠ 0 := fun h0 => hj fun _ => h0
          have hjtop : hom (f j) (l j, y j) = ⊤ := by
            rw [hj0, hom_apply_zero, smulRight_zero (hp j).convexDom_nonempty,
              indicatorFn_of_notMem (by simpa using hjy)]
          have htop : (⨆ i, hom (f i) (l i, y i)) = ⊤ :=
            top_le_iff.1 (le_iSup_of_le j hjtop.ge)
          rw [htop]
          exact le_top
      · obtain ⟨j, hj⟩ := not_forall.1 hpos
        have htop : (⨆ i, hom (f i) (l i, y i)) = ⊤ :=
          top_le_iff.1 (le_iSup_of_le j (hom_apply_neg (not_le.1 hj) (f j) (y j)).ge)
        rw [htop]
        exact le_top
  rwa [hkey] at hslice

end Rockafellar
